      "heading": "Seven ways to write site inspection reports faster",
      "html": "<ol><li><strong>One template, every time.</strong> The same six sections in the same order. You fill facts in, you do not design a report.</li><li><strong>Say it at the spot.</strong> A sentence spoken in front of the fault beats a note decoded at 6pm.</li><li><strong>One photo per finding, taken then.</strong> Name or caption it as you take it, so nobody matches photos later.</li><li><strong>Write the action with the finding.</strong> What, who, by when. Leave it for later and it gets skipped.</li><li><strong>Keep a bank of standard wording</strong> for the findings you see every week, then edit for the site.</li><li><strong>Record only what changed</strong> on repeat visits, and mark the rest as checked.</li><li><strong>Finish on site.</strong> Ten minutes in the car park is quicker than an hour that evening, and truer.</li></ol>"
