      "heading": "Questions, answered",
      "html": "<h3>How can I write site inspection reports faster?</h3><p>Use one fixed template, say each finding as you stand at it, take one photo per finding, write each action with an owner and a date there and then, and finish before you leave site. Tools such as dictation, voice-to-report apps or Quickler can do the typing.</p><h3>How long should a site inspection report take to write?</h3><p>There is no set time. It depends on the site and the template. As one measured example, Graeme Clark, an H&amp;S consultant at Ethos Safety, went from 1 to 2 hours of write-up per report to 14 minutes on average.</p><h3>What must a site inspection report include?</h3><p>The site, the date and time, who inspected, what you covered and what you did not, how you did it, your findings with photos, actions with an owner and a date, and your sign-off.</p><h3>What is the difference between a finding and an observation?</h3><p>An observation is what you saw. A finding is an observation judged against a standard, as compliant or not. A good report keeps the two apart.</p><h3>How specific should observations be?</h3><p>Specific enough that someone who was not there knows what and where. \"Deteriorating condition\" is too vague. \"About 3m of spalling brickwork, north side, first floor, mortar missing. Photo 7\" is right.</p><h3>Can I use voice notes for observations?</h3><p>Yes. You speak what you see as you stand in front of it. Quickler writes the words out in the WhatsApp chat, so you do not type.</p><h3>How soon after the visit should a site inspection report be written?</h3><p>For a working platform used for construction work, regulation 12(7) of the Work at Height Regulations 2005 says the person who inspects prepares the report before the end of the working period in which the inspection is completed, and gives it to the person it was done for within 24 hours (legislation.gov.uk, checked 18 November 2026). For other site reports, check the standard or contract that asks for it. Writing it on the same visit keeps the detail.</p><h3>Who signs a site inspection report?</h3><p>The person who carried out the inspection. For a scaffold report, Schedule 7 of the Work at Height Regulations 2005 asks for that person's name and position. Some contracts and certificates ask for a qualification or a second signature, so check yours.</p>"
